# Plan Atlas

Plan Atlas is the single store for implementation plans. Use this skill any time a plan is created or updated, and any time you execute a task from a plan.

## Rules

- ALWAYS use the `plan-atlas` MCP tools. Never write plan files yourself (not in the repo, not in `~/.plan-atlas`, not in `~/.claude/plans/`).
- Before `create_plan`: call `get_taxonomy` (tags must come from it) and `search_plans` with the plan's topic and likely tags. List every relevant plan you find under `related`.
- Revise an existing plan with `update_plan` (send the full Markdown). Use `list_plans` / `get_plan` to read plans.
- If a tool returns `warnings`, fix them with `update_plan`.
- Only use tags from the taxonomy. If none fits, do not invent one: propose it in the plan text as `new/<tag>` (for example in the Overview) and leave it out of `tags`.

## Required structure

```markdown
---
title: <title>
tags: [type/feature, domain/backend, tech/jwt]
related:
  - { to: PLAN-auth-001, type: depends_on }   # depends_on | extends | relates_to
---

## 01. Overview
## 02. Requirements & Scope
## 03. Technical Analysis
### Current Architecture
### Existing Problems
### Proposed Architecture
## 04. Task Breakdown
### TASK-001: <name>
- complexity: 1-5
- model: haiku | sonnet | opus
- effort: low | medium | high
- depends_on: [TASK-000]
- status: todo
- tags: [domain/backend]
<description and acceptance criteria>
## 05. Risks & Rollback
```

`id`, `project` and `created` are assigned by the server. Number tasks `TASK-001`, `TASK-002`, ...

Relations: `depends_on` = this plan needs the other finished first; `extends` = builds on the other plan's feature; `relates_to` = same topic, no ordering.

## Complexity → model and effort

Score every task 1–5. `model`/`effort` may be omitted; they default from complexity.

| Score | Signals | Model | Effort |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Text or config edit, one file | haiku | low |
| 2 | CRUD following an existing pattern | sonnet | low |
| 3 | Several files, needs tests | sonnet | medium |
| 4 | Architecture change, security, concurrency | opus | medium |
| 5 | New design, ambiguous, data-loss risk | opus | high |

## Executing tasks

1. Right before working on a task, call `start_task` (plan_id, task_id).
2. When it is finished and verified, call `complete_task`.
3. If the MCP tools are unavailable, run `plan-atlas task start <PLAN-ID> <TASK-ID>` and `plan-atlas task done <PLAN-ID> <TASK-ID>` instead.
4. When dispatching a subagent for a task, use that task's model and effort (from `get_plan`: the task's explicit `model`/`effort`, otherwise the complexity default above; a task without complexity counts as 3).

Activity of the session and its subagents is captured by hooks automatically; nothing else to do.

